WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:19.193
I am the proud father
of two beautiful children,

00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:23.535
Elijah, 15, and Octavia, 12.

00:00:24.313 --> 00:00:27.630
When Elijah was in the fourth grade,

00:00:27.654 --> 00:00:29.218
he came to me,

00:00:29.242 --> 00:00:32.103
came home from school
bubbling over with excitement

00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:37.046
about what he had learned that day
about African-American history.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:41.502
Now, I'm an African-American
and cultural studies professor,

00:00:41.526 --> 00:00:43.667
and so, as you can imagine,

00:00:43.691 --> 00:00:46.618
African-American culture
is kind of serious around my home.

00:00:46.642 --> 00:00:50.888
So I was very proud that my son
was excited about what he had learned

00:00:50.912 --> 00:00:52.063
that day in school.

00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:54.027
So I said, "What did you learn?"

00:00:54.051 --> 00:00:57.529
He said, "I learned about Rosa Parks."

00:00:57.907 --> 00:01:00.959
I said, "OK, what did you learn
about Rosa Parks?"

00:01:00.983 --> 00:01:06.497
He said, "I learned that Rosa Parks
was this frail, old black woman

00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:08.371
in the 1950s

00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:10.829
in Montgomery, Alabama.

00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:13.103
And she sat down on this bus,

00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:15.095
and she had tired feet,

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:20.052
and when the bus driver told her
to give up her seat to a white patron,

00:01:20.076 --> 00:01:22.752
she refused because she had tired feet.

00:01:22.776 --> 00:01:24.041
It had been a long day,

00:01:24.065 --> 00:01:25.883
and she was tired of oppression,

00:01:25.907 --> 00:01:27.796
and she didn't give up her seat.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.749
And she marched with Martin Luther King,

00:01:29.773 --> 00:01:31.965
and she believed in nonviolence."

00:01:31.989 --> 00:01:35.153
And I guess he must have looked at my face

00:01:35.177 --> 00:01:39.707
and saw that I was
a little less than impressed

00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:41.064
by his

00:01:41.484 --> 00:01:42.704
... um ...

00:01:42.754 --> 00:01:44.084
history lesson.

00:01:44.134 --> 00:01:48.788
And so he stopped, and he was like,
"Dad, what's wrong? What did I get wrong?"

00:01:48.812 --> 00:01:50.901
I said, "Son, you didn't
get anything wrong,

00:01:50.925 --> 00:01:53.685
but I think your teacher
got a whole lot of things wrong."

00:01:53.709 --> 00:01:54.719
(Laughter)

00:01:54.743 --> 00:01:56.415
He said, "Well, what do you mean?"

00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:59.679
I said, "Rosa Parks was not tired.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.329
She was not old.

00:02:03.353 --> 00:02:06.409
And she certainly didn't have tired feet."

00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:07.597
He said, "What?"

00:02:07.621 --> 00:02:08.779
I said, "Yes!

00:02:08.803 --> 00:02:10.998
Rosa Parks was only 42 years old" --

00:02:12.261 --> 00:02:14.605
Yeah, you're shocked, right?
Never heard that.

00:02:14.629 --> 00:02:16.586
"Rosa Parks was only 42 years old,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.354
she had only worked six hours that day,
and she was a seamstress

00:02:21.378 --> 00:02:23.746
and her feet were just fine.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:24.901
(Laughter)

00:02:24.925 --> 00:02:27.624
The only thing that she was tired of

00:02:27.648 --> 00:02:30.071
was she was tired of inequality.

00:02:30.095 --> 00:02:32.161
She was tired of oppression."

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:33.473
And my son said,

00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:37.455
"Well, why would my teacher
tell me this thing?

00:02:37.479 --> 00:02:39.353
This is confusing for me."

00:02:39.377 --> 00:02:42.779
Because he loved his teacher,
and she was a good teacher,

00:02:42.803 --> 00:02:45.746
a young-ish, 20-something white woman,

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:49.481
really, really smart, pushed him,
so I liked her as well.

00:02:49.505 --> 00:02:52.498
But he was confused.
"Why would she tell me this?" he said.

00:02:52.522 --> 00:02:56.033
He said, "Dad, tell me more. Tell me more.
Tell me more about Rosa Parks."

00:02:56.057 --> 00:02:58.310
And I said, "Son, I'll do you one better."

00:02:58.884 --> 00:03:00.037
He was like, "What?"

00:03:00.061 --> 00:03:02.121
I said, "I'm going to buy
her autobiography,

00:03:02.145 --> 00:03:04.183
and I'm going to let you
read it yourself."

00:03:04.207 --> 00:03:06.899
(Laughter)

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.434
So as you can imagine,

00:03:10.453 --> 00:03:15.090
Elijah wasn't too excited about
this new, lengthy homework assignment

00:03:15.114 --> 00:03:18.892
that his dad had just given him,
but he took it in stride.

00:03:19.451 --> 00:03:23.097
And he came back after he had read it,

00:03:23.121 --> 00:03:27.039
and he was excited
about what he had learned.

00:03:27.063 --> 00:03:34.062
He said, "Dad, not only was Rosa Parks
not initially into nonviolence,

00:03:34.086 --> 00:03:38.622
but Rosa Parks's grandfather,
who basically raised her

00:03:38.646 --> 00:03:40.806
and was light enough to pass as white,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:45.470
used to walk around town
with his gun in his holster,

00:03:45.494 --> 00:03:50.449
and people knew if you messed with
Mr. Parks's children or grandchildren,

00:03:50.473 --> 00:03:54.684
he would put a cap
in your proverbial bottom."

00:03:54.708 --> 00:03:55.910
(Laughter)

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:57.094
Right?

00:03:57.118 --> 00:03:59.437
He was not someone to mess with.

00:03:59.922 --> 00:04:05.790
And he said, "I also learned
that Rosa Parks married a man in Raymond

00:04:05.814 --> 00:04:08.977
who was a lot like her grandfather."

00:04:09.884 --> 00:04:11.329
He would organize.

00:04:11.353 --> 00:04:13.669
He was a civil rights activist.

00:04:13.693 --> 00:04:17.308
He would organize events

00:04:17.332 --> 00:04:22.271
and sometimes the events would be
at Rosa Parks's home.

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:24.315
And one time Rosa Parks remarked

00:04:24.339 --> 00:04:26.654
that there were so many guns on the table,

00:04:26.678 --> 00:04:29.946
because they were prepared for somebody
to come busting into the door

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.733
that they were prepared
for whatever was going to go down,

00:04:32.757 --> 00:04:35.587
that Rosa Parks said, "There were
so many guns on the table

00:04:35.611 --> 00:04:38.340
that I forgot to even
offer them coffee or food."

00:04:38.832 --> 00:04:41.403
This is who Rosa Parks was.

00:04:41.427 --> 00:04:46.113
And in fact, Rosa Parks,
when she was sitting on that bus that day,

00:04:46.137 --> 00:04:48.775
waiting for those
police officers to arrive

00:04:48.799 --> 00:04:51.746
and not knowing what was going
to happen to her,

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.927
she was not thinking about
Martin Luther King,

00:04:53.951 --> 00:04:55.407
who she barely knew.

00:04:55.431 --> 00:04:58.271
She was not thinking about
nonviolence or Gandhi.

00:04:58.295 --> 00:05:00.226
She was thinking about her grandfather,

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.276
a gun-toting, take-no-mess grandfather.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.838
That's who Rosa Parks was thinking about.

00:05:07.862 --> 00:05:11.536
My son was mesmerized by Rosa Parks,

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.507
and I was proud of him
to see this excitement.

00:05:15.125 --> 00:05:17.183
But then I still had a problem.

00:05:17.207 --> 00:05:19.762
Because I still had to go his school

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:21.793
and address the issue with his teacher,

00:05:21.817 --> 00:05:25.116
because I didn't want her
to continue to teach the kids

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.512
obviously false history.

00:05:27.885 --> 00:05:29.316
So I'm agonizing over this,

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.800
primarily because I understand,
as an African-American man,

00:05:32.824 --> 00:05:35.168
that whenever you talk
to whites about racism

00:05:35.192 --> 00:05:37.197
or anything that's racially sensitive,

00:05:37.221 --> 00:05:39.179
there's usually going to be a challenge.

00:05:39.203 --> 00:05:45.263
This is what white sociologist
Robin DiAngelo calls "white fragility."

00:05:45.287 --> 00:05:47.084
She argues that, in fact,

00:05:47.108 --> 00:05:51.147
because whites have
so little experience being challenged

00:05:51.171 --> 00:05:52.644
about their white privilege

00:05:52.668 --> 00:05:56.163
that whenever even the most
minute challenge is brought before them,

00:05:56.187 --> 00:05:58.010
they usually cry,

00:05:58.034 --> 00:05:59.185
get angry

00:05:59.209 --> 00:06:00.360
or run.

00:06:00.384 --> 00:06:01.389
(Laughter)

00:06:01.413 --> 00:06:04.400
And I have experienced them all.

00:06:04.424 --> 00:06:10.236
And so, when I was contemplating
confronting his teacher,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.833
I wasn't happy about it,

00:06:11.857 --> 00:06:13.920
but I was like, this is a necessary evil

00:06:13.944 --> 00:06:18.246
of being a black parent trying to raise
self-actualized black children.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.947
So I called Elijah to me and said,

00:06:19.971 --> 00:06:24.744
"Elijah, I'm going to set up
an appointment with your teacher

00:06:24.768 --> 00:06:25.976
and try and correct this

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.237
and maybe your principal.

00:06:27.261 --> 00:06:28.662
What do you think?"

00:06:28.686 --> 00:06:29.837
And Elijah said,

00:06:29.861 --> 00:06:31.997
"Dad, I have a better idea."

00:06:33.021 --> 00:06:34.974
And I said, "Really? What's your idea?"

00:06:34.998 --> 00:06:39.628
He said, "We have
a public speaking assignment,

00:06:39.652 --> 00:06:42.217
and why don't I use
that public speaking assignment

00:06:42.241 --> 00:06:45.804
to talk about debunking
the myths of Rosa Parks?"

00:06:46.756 --> 00:06:47.913
And I was like,

00:06:48.815 --> 00:06:50.262
"Well, that is a good idea."

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:55.083
So Elijah goes to school,

00:06:55.107 --> 00:06:56.886
he does his presentation,

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.404
he comes back home,

00:06:58.428 --> 00:07:00.801
and I could see something
positive happened.

00:07:00.825 --> 00:07:02.736
I said, "Well, what happened, son?"

00:07:03.231 --> 00:07:05.411
He said, "Well, later on in that day,

00:07:05.435 --> 00:07:07.379
the teacher pulled me aside,

00:07:07.403 --> 00:07:11.999
and she apologized to me
for giving that misinformation."

00:07:12.771 --> 00:07:16.299
And then something else
miraculous happened the next day.

00:07:16.323 --> 00:07:20.032
She actually taught
a new lesson on Rosa Parks,

00:07:20.056 --> 00:07:24.370
filling in the gaps that she had left
and correcting the mistakes that she made.

00:07:24.394 --> 00:07:28.988
And I was so, so proud of my son.

00:07:29.956 --> 00:07:32.644
But then I thought about it.

00:07:33.875 --> 00:07:35.090
And I got angry.

00:07:35.565 --> 00:07:37.121
And I got real angry.

00:07:38.344 --> 00:07:40.062
Why? Why would I get angry?

00:07:40.878 --> 00:07:45.852
Because my nine-year-old son
had to educate his teacher

00:07:45.876 --> 00:07:47.367
about his history,

00:07:47.391 --> 00:07:50.441
had to educate his teacher
about his own humanity.

00:07:50.465 --> 00:07:52.134
He's nine years old.

00:07:52.784 --> 00:07:56.001
He should be thinking about
basketball or soccer

00:07:56.025 --> 00:07:58.496
or the latest movie.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:02.687
He should not be thinking about
having to take the responsibility

00:08:02.711 --> 00:08:05.074
of educating his teacher,

00:08:05.098 --> 00:08:08.161
his students,

00:08:08.185 --> 00:08:10.652
about himself, about his history.

00:08:10.676 --> 00:08:12.303
That was a burden that I carried.

00:08:12.327 --> 00:08:14.350
That was a burden that my parents carried

00:08:14.374 --> 00:08:16.104
and generations before them carried.

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:20.945
And now I was seeing my son
take on that burden, too.

00:08:21.513 --> 00:08:26.443
You see, that's why Rosa Parks
wrote her autobiography.

00:08:26.467 --> 00:08:28.205
Because during her lifetime,

00:08:28.229 --> 00:08:29.745
if you can imagine,

00:08:29.769 --> 00:08:34.384
you do this amazing thing,

00:08:34.408 --> 00:08:38.327
you're alive and you're talking
about your civil rights activism,

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:40.284
and a story emerges

00:08:40.308 --> 00:08:43.199
in which somebody is telling the world

00:08:43.223 --> 00:08:45.863
that you were old and you had tired feet

00:08:45.887 --> 00:08:48.208
and you just were an accidental activist,

00:08:48.232 --> 00:08:52.093
not that you had been activist
by then for 20 years,

00:08:52.117 --> 00:08:55.491
not that the boycott
had been planned for months,

00:08:55.515 --> 00:08:59.986
not that you were not even the first
or the second or even the third woman

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:01.867
to be arrested for doing that.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:07.992
You become an accidental activist,
even in her own lifetime.

00:09:08.016 --> 00:09:11.146
So she wrote that autobiography
to correct the record,

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:14.055
because what she wanted
to remind people of

00:09:15.468 --> 00:09:17.567
was that this

00:09:17.591 --> 00:09:18.782
is what it was like

00:09:19.813 --> 00:09:22.984
in the 1950s

00:09:23.008 --> 00:09:25.725
trying to be black in America

00:09:25.749 --> 00:09:28.087
and fight for your rights.

00:09:28.561 --> 00:09:32.068
During the year, a little over a year,
that the boycott lasted,

00:09:33.298 --> 00:09:35.927
there were over four church bombings.

00:09:35.951 --> 00:09:38.955
Martin Luther King's house
was bombed twice.

00:09:39.392 --> 00:09:42.865
Other civil rights leaders' houses
were bombed in Birmingham.

00:09:43.587 --> 00:09:48.855
Rosa Parks's husband
slept at night with a shotgun,

00:09:48.879 --> 00:09:51.146
because they would get
constant death threats.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.676
In fact, Rosa Parks's mother
lived with them,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.176
and sometimes she would stay
on the phone for hours

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.360
so that nobody would call in
with death threats,

00:09:59.384 --> 00:10:01.609
because it was constant and persistent.

00:10:01.633 --> 00:10:04.260
In fact, there was so much tension,

00:10:04.284 --> 00:10:06.941
there was so much pressure,
there was so much terrorism,

00:10:06.965 --> 00:10:09.492
that Rosa Parks and her husband,
they lost their jobs,

00:10:09.516 --> 00:10:11.330
and they became unemployable

00:10:11.354 --> 00:10:15.456
and eventually had to leave
and move out of the South.

00:10:17.558 --> 00:10:20.512
This is a civil rights reality

00:10:20.536 --> 00:10:25.154
that Rosa Parks wanted to make sure
that people understood.

00:10:25.981 --> 00:10:31.552
So you say, "Well, David,
what does that have to do with me?

00:10:31.576 --> 00:10:33.506
I'm a well-meaning person.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.064
I didn't own slaves.

00:10:35.088 --> 00:10:36.843
I'm not trying to whitewash history.

00:10:36.867 --> 00:10:39.155
I'm a good guy. I'm a good person."

00:10:40.433 --> 00:10:42.446
Let me tell you
what it has to do with you,

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:44.934
and I'll tell it to you
by telling you a story

00:10:44.958 --> 00:10:48.485
about a professor of mine,
a white professor,

00:10:48.509 --> 00:10:52.874
when I was in graduate school,
who was a brilliant, brilliant individual.

00:10:52.898 --> 00:10:54.176
We'll call him "Fred."

00:10:55.061 --> 00:10:59.710
And Fred was writing this history
of the civil rights movement,

00:10:59.734 --> 00:11:02.215
but he was writing specifically
about a moment

00:11:02.239 --> 00:11:04.151
that happened to him in North Carolina

00:11:04.175 --> 00:11:08.438
when this white man shot this black man
in cold blood in a wide-open space

00:11:08.462 --> 00:11:09.995
and was never convicted.

00:11:10.769 --> 00:11:12.537
And so it was this great book,

00:11:12.561 --> 00:11:16.273
and he called together
a couple of his professor friends

00:11:16.297 --> 00:11:20.878
and he called me to read a draft of it
before the final submission.

00:11:20.902 --> 00:11:22.744
And I was flattered that he called me;

00:11:22.768 --> 00:11:24.488
I was only a graduate student then.

00:11:24.512 --> 00:11:28.420
I was kind of feeling myself a little bit.
I was like, "OK, yeah."

00:11:28.444 --> 00:11:31.014
I'm sitting around amongst intellectuals,

00:11:31.038 --> 00:11:35.540
and I read the draft of the book.

00:11:35.564 --> 00:11:37.559
And there was a moment in the book

00:11:37.583 --> 00:11:40.226
that struck me as being
deeply problematic,

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:41.415
and so I said,

00:11:41.439 --> 00:11:45.015
"Fred," as we were sitting around
talking about this draft,

00:11:45.039 --> 00:11:49.726
I said, "Fred, I've got a real problem
with this moment that you talk

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.968
about your maid in your book."

00:11:52.992 --> 00:11:59.685
And I could see Fred get a little
"tight," as we say.

00:11:59.709 --> 00:12:03.472
He said, "What do you mean?
That's a great story.

00:12:03.496 --> 00:12:05.347
It happened just like I said."

00:12:05.371 --> 00:12:08.281
I said, "Mmm ... can I give you
another scenario?"

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:10.279
Now, what's the story?

00:12:10.303 --> 00:12:11.920
It was 1968.

00:12:12.649 --> 00:12:15.785
Martin Luther King
had just been assassinated.

00:12:16.443 --> 00:12:20.580
His maid, "domestic" --
we'll call her "Mabel,"

00:12:20.604 --> 00:12:21.809
was in the kitchen.

00:12:22.706 --> 00:12:24.312
Little Fred is eight years old.

00:12:24.336 --> 00:12:26.177
Little Fred comes into the kitchen,

00:12:26.968 --> 00:12:33.119
and Mabel, who he has only seen
as smiling and helpful and happy,

00:12:33.143 --> 00:12:35.425
is bent over the sink,

00:12:35.449 --> 00:12:36.859
and she's crying,

00:12:37.896 --> 00:12:39.311
and she's sobbing

00:12:40.479 --> 00:12:41.684
inconsolably.

00:12:42.604 --> 00:12:46.781
And little Fred comes over to her
and says, "Mabel, what is wrong?"

00:12:47.941 --> 00:12:49.980
Mabel turns, and she says,

00:12:50.004 --> 00:12:54.550
"They killed him! They killed our leader.
They killed Martin Luther King.

00:12:54.574 --> 00:12:57.916
He's dead! They are monsters."

00:12:59.054 --> 00:13:00.873
And little Fred says,

00:13:00.897 --> 00:13:04.222
"It'll be OK, Mabel.
It'll be OK. It'll be OK."

00:13:04.246 --> 00:13:07.345
And she looked at him, and she says,
"No, it's not going to be OK.

00:13:07.369 --> 00:13:09.311
Did you not hear what I just said?

00:13:09.335 --> 00:13:11.614
They killed Martin Luther King."

00:13:13.106 --> 00:13:15.087
And Fred,

00:13:15.111 --> 00:13:16.484
son of a preacher,

00:13:17.882 --> 00:13:20.344
looks up at Mabel, and he says,

00:13:20.368 --> 00:13:25.369
"But Mabel, didn't Jesus
die on the cross for our sins?

00:13:25.784 --> 00:13:27.326
Wasn't that a good outcome?

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:30.742
Maybe this will be a good outcome.

00:13:30.766 --> 00:13:35.399
Maybe the death of Martin Luther King
will lead to a good outcome."

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:38.374
And as Fred tells the story,

00:13:38.398 --> 00:13:42.140
he says that Mabel
put her hand over her mouth,

00:13:43.417 --> 00:13:45.986
she reached down
and she gave little Fred a hug,

00:13:47.002 --> 00:13:49.579
and then she reached into the icebox,

00:13:49.603 --> 00:13:51.879
and took out a couple Pepsis,

00:13:51.903 --> 00:13:53.359
gave him some Pepsis

00:13:53.383 --> 00:13:56.228
and sent him on his way
to play with his siblings.

00:13:56.948 --> 00:13:58.511
And he said,

00:13:58.535 --> 00:14:04.169
"This was proof that even in the most
harrowing times of race struggle

00:14:04.193 --> 00:14:07.445
that two people could come together
across racial lines

00:14:07.469 --> 00:14:09.665
and find human commonality

00:14:09.689 --> 00:14:12.359
along the lines of love and affection."

00:14:12.383 --> 00:14:16.228
And I said, "Fred, that is some BS."

00:14:16.252 --> 00:14:18.252
(Laughter)

00:14:18.803 --> 00:14:20.179
(Applause)

00:14:20.203 --> 00:14:23.155
Fred was like,

00:14:23.179 --> 00:14:26.752
"But I don't understand, David.
That's the story."

00:14:26.776 --> 00:14:28.930
I said, "Fred, let me ask you a question."

00:14:29.732 --> 00:14:35.951
I said, "You were
in North Carolina in 1968.

00:14:35.975 --> 00:14:39.280
If Mabel would've went to her community --
you were eight years old --

00:14:39.304 --> 00:14:42.222
what do you think the eight-year-old
African-American children

00:14:42.246 --> 00:14:43.397
were calling her?

00:14:43.421 --> 00:14:45.668
Do you think they called her
by her first name?"

00:14:45.692 --> 00:14:47.272
No, they called her "Miss Mabel,"

00:14:47.296 --> 00:14:50.700
or they called her "Miss Johnson,"
or they called her "Auntie Johnson."

00:14:50.724 --> 00:14:53.311
They would have never dared
call her by her first name,

00:14:53.335 --> 00:14:55.867
because that would have been
the height of disrespect.

00:14:55.891 --> 00:14:58.125
And yet, you were calling
her by her first name

00:14:58.149 --> 00:14:59.733
every single day that she worked,

00:14:59.757 --> 00:15:01.685
and you never thought about it."

00:15:01.709 --> 00:15:04.718
I said, "Let me ask you another question:
Was Mabel married?

00:15:05.255 --> 00:15:06.729
Did she have children?

00:15:06.753 --> 00:15:08.421
What church did she go to?

00:15:08.445 --> 00:15:10.675
What was her favorite dessert?"

00:15:12.754 --> 00:15:16.667
Fred could not answer
any of those questions.

00:15:17.337 --> 00:15:20.828
I said, "Fred, this story
is not about Mabel.

00:15:20.852 --> 00:15:22.241
This story is about you."

00:15:22.883 --> 00:15:25.850
I said, "This story made you feel good,

00:15:25.874 --> 00:15:28.033
but this story is not about Mabel.

00:15:28.057 --> 00:15:29.651
The reality is,

00:15:29.675 --> 00:15:32.229
what probably happened was,
Mabel was crying,

00:15:32.253 --> 00:15:34.329
which was not something
she customarily did,

00:15:34.353 --> 00:15:36.118
so she was letting her guard down.

00:15:36.637 --> 00:15:38.269
And you came into the kitchen,

00:15:38.293 --> 00:15:41.790
and you caught her at a weak moment
where she was letting her guard down.

00:15:42.278 --> 00:15:45.764
And see, because you thought of yourself
as just like one of her children,

00:15:45.788 --> 00:15:50.470
you didn't recognize that you
were in fact the child of her employer.

00:15:50.899 --> 00:15:53.263
And she'd found herself yelling at you.

00:15:53.287 --> 00:15:54.661
And then she caught herself,

00:15:54.685 --> 00:15:57.057
realizing that, 'If I'm yelling at him

00:15:57.081 --> 00:15:59.658
and he goes back and he tells
his dad or he tells mom,

00:15:59.682 --> 00:16:02.104
I could lose my job.'

00:16:02.934 --> 00:16:05.223
And so she tempered herself,
and she ended up --

00:16:05.247 --> 00:16:09.845
even though she needed consoling --
she ended up consoling you

00:16:09.869 --> 00:16:11.498
and sending you on your way,

00:16:11.522 --> 00:16:15.197
perhaps so she could finish
mourning in peace."

00:16:16.033 --> 00:16:17.298
And Fred was stunned.

00:16:17.782 --> 00:16:21.802
And he realized that he had actually
misread that moment.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.590
And see, this is what
they did to Rosa Parks.

00:16:25.614 --> 00:16:31.040
Because it's a lot easier to digest
an old grandmother with tired feet

00:16:31.064 --> 00:16:34.485
who doesn't stand up because
she wants to fight for inequality,

00:16:34.509 --> 00:16:36.800
but because her feet
and her back are tired,

00:16:36.824 --> 00:16:38.462
and she's worked all day.

00:16:39.269 --> 00:16:42.074
See, old grandmothers are not scary.

00:16:42.098 --> 00:16:44.250
But young, radical black women

00:16:44.274 --> 00:16:46.415
who don't take any stuff from anybody

00:16:46.439 --> 00:16:47.943
are very scary,

00:16:47.967 --> 00:16:49.853
who stand up to power

00:16:49.877 --> 00:16:51.941
and are willing to die for that --

00:16:51.965 --> 00:16:54.240
those are not the kind of people

00:16:54.264 --> 00:16:57.024
that make us comfortable.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:00.441
So you say,

00:17:01.258 --> 00:17:02.978
"What do you want me to do, David?

00:17:03.002 --> 00:17:06.262
I don't know what to do."

00:17:07.159 --> 00:17:09.656
Well, what I would say to you is,

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:11.725
there was a time in which,

00:17:11.749 --> 00:17:13.965
if you were Jewish, you were not white,

00:17:13.989 --> 00:17:15.997
if you were Italian, you were not white,

00:17:16.021 --> 00:17:17.919
if you were Irish, you were not white

00:17:17.943 --> 00:17:19.105
in this country.

00:17:19.129 --> 00:17:24.137
It took a while before the Irish,
the Jews and the Italians became white.

00:17:24.843 --> 00:17:26.014
Right?

00:17:26.038 --> 00:17:28.189
There was a time in which
you were "othered,"

00:17:28.213 --> 00:17:31.193
when you were the people on the outside.

00:17:33.736 --> 00:17:35.016
Toni Morrison said,

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:38.765
"If, in order for you to be tall,
I have to be on my knees,

00:17:38.789 --> 00:17:40.163
you have a serious problem."

00:17:40.187 --> 00:17:43.305
She says, "White America
has a serious, serious problem."

00:17:44.583 --> 00:17:49.596
To be honest, I don't know
if race relations will improve in America.

00:17:50.127 --> 00:17:51.922
But I know that if they will improve,

00:17:51.946 --> 00:17:55.904
we have to take
these challenges on head on.

00:17:56.619 --> 00:17:58.613
The future of my children depends on it.

00:17:58.637 --> 00:18:01.209
The future of my children's
children depends on it.

00:18:01.731 --> 00:18:03.622
And, whether you know it or not,

00:18:03.646 --> 00:18:07.812
the future of your children
and your children's children

00:18:07.836 --> 00:18:09.119
depends on it, too.

00:18:09.632 --> 00:18:10.786
Thank you.

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:12.205
(Applause)

